# FIPS DLL staging in `build.rs` (workspace-wide rule)

Every binary crate in the offline workspace that depends on
`aws-lc-rs = { features = ["fips"] }` (transitively or directly) imports
`aws_lc_fips_<version>_crypto.dll` (currently
`aws_lc_fips_0_13_14_crypto.dll`) at runtime. Cargo builds this DLL via
the `aws-lc-fips-sys` build script and emits a
`cargo:rustc-link-search` pointing at the build directory so the import
library is found at link time -- but Cargo does **NOT** copy the DLL
next to the produced binary.

The MSI installer scripts under `pim-installers/` copy the DLL into
`%ProgramFiles%\AIC\OfflinePimServer\` / `OfflinePimAgent\` for shipped
artifacts, so installed copies work. But `cargo run`,
`cargo test`, `playwright`-driven E2E tests, and a developer launching
`target\release\<bin>.exe` directly all fail on Windows with:

```
The code execution cannot proceed because aws_lc_fips_0_13_14_crypto.dll
was not found. Reinstalling the program may fix this problem.
```

## Required pattern

Every binary crate (and every cdylib that ships as an executable
component) under `c:\analog-pim\*` that links `aws-lc-rs/fips` MUST have
a `build.rs` containing the **`stage_aws_lc_fips_dll`** helper. The
canonical copy lives in
`pim-offline-server-configurator-win32/build.rs`; the same helper is
duplicated byte-for-byte across every consumer because we don't
currently have a shared `[build-dependencies]` crate reachable from each
repo. Keep the copies in sync if you change one.

The helper:

1. Finds the most recently modified
   `target/<profile>/build/aws-lc-fips-sys-*/out/build/artifacts/aws_lc_fips_*_crypto.dll`
   (scans every hash-suffixed sibling, because an incremental build may
   leave the "live" hash dir empty while the DLL still exists in an
   older sibling).
2. Copies it to `target/<profile>/<dll>` for direct execution +
   `cargo run`.
3. Copies it to `target/<profile>/deps/<dll>` for `cargo test` test
   binaries.
4. Is idempotent (size + mtime check before copying), gated on
   `#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]`, and a no-op on non-Windows.
5. Emits `cargo:warning=...` lines on every recoverable failure --
   never panics (matches `.cursor/rules/no-panic-enterprise.mdc`).

