## How to verify

Before merging a change that adds a new FIPS-linked binary, or after
touching the helper:

```powershell
# Clean the staged DLL to force the build.rs path
Remove-Item target\release\aws_lc_fips_*_crypto.dll -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Remove-Item target\release\deps\aws_lc_fips_*_crypto.dll -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
(Get-Item build.rs).LastWriteTime = Get-Date  # force re-run

cargo build --release
Test-Path target\release\aws_lc_fips_0_13_14_crypto.dll
Test-Path target\release\deps\aws_lc_fips_0_13_14_crypto.dll

# Optional: confirm cargo test still works
cargo test --release --lib
```

Both `Test-Path` calls should return `True`. Crypto tests should pass.

## Why not a shared helper crate

A `[build-dependencies]` crate would deduplicate the ~80 lines of
helper, but every consumer repo would then need a path-patch to find it
(the workspace is multi-repo, not a single Cargo workspace). The
maintenance cost of cross-repo path patches has been higher than the
cost of keeping eight near-identical `build.rs` blocks in sync. Revisit
this trade-off if a third copy needs to change for a different reason.

## Related rules

- `.cursor/rules/enterprise-compliance.mdc` §4 -- NIST 800-53 SC-13;
  the FIPS DLL is the validated cryptographic module boundary.
- `.cursor/rules/no-panic-enterprise.mdc` -- the helper warns and
  returns; it never panics.
- Per-product MSI scripts under `pim-installers/scripts/` -- those
  remain the source of truth for the **installed** copy of the DLL;
  this rule covers the **developer / CI / test** workflow.
